          "guid": "msmlij2ej7r03r1j",
          "message": {
            "text": "The release pitch now leads with all three headline capabilities and the transcript includes the initial waiting and failed-condition lines that the real reporter emits; omitting them made the old screenshot-like example stale.",
            "markdown": "The release pitch now leads with all three headline capabilities and the transcript includes the initial waiting and failed-condition lines that the real reporter emits; omitting them made the old screenshot-like example stale."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "README.md"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 3,
                  "endLine": 20,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "`pueue wait`, plus **timeouts**, **script conditions**, and **machine-readable\nresults**.\n\n[pueue](https://github.com/Nukesor/pueue) can wait for tasks to finish. It cannot\ngive up after a while, and it cannot stop early because *something else* became\ntrue. `pueue-wait-cond` does both, emits stable JSON when a script needs the\nanswer, and turns a typoed task id into a bounded failure instead of a hang.\n\n```console\n$ pueue-wait-cond 42 --timeout 300 --until ./deploy-is-live.sh\n09:20:07 - Waiting on 1 task(s): 42 (Running)\n09:20:07 - --until condition \"./deploy-is-live.sh\" exit 1\n09:20:09 - --until condition \"./deploy-is-live.sh\" satisfied; done waiting\n```\n\nThe normal output stays compatible with `pueue wait`. Add `--json` for one\nparseable result object, or `--task-grace forever` when you deliberately want\nthe old unbounded wait for a task id that has not appeared yet."
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
